Two slag-lignin mixtures (compressed briquettes and powder) were used in the experiment as shown in Table  I . In this work BOF slag was obtained from steel plant in Finland, and hydrolysis lignin was used as a biomass which is the main byproduct in paper and pulp industry. [ 22 ]   The slag and lignin were ground using a ball mill and then mixed in a pre-calculated stoichiometric ratio, ensuring that the fixed carbon was sufficient to reduce all iron-bearing oxides. Briquettes were prepared by using an A ZYP-20TS Automatic Powder Tablet Press (Xiamen Tmax Battery Equipments Limited, Xiamen, China) with a 20 mm diameter. The applied force (4T) was calculated based on the powder sample's diameter (20 mm) and the required average compressive pressure (160Mpa). The BOF slag sample was mixed with lignin according to the stoichiometric ratios of total Fe +2 , Fe +3 , and fixed carbon content. The experiment was carried out as shown in Table  I . The main expected reduction reaction is as shown in Equation  1 . 1 Ca 2 Fe 2 O 5 ( s ) + C ( s ) = Fe ( s ) + CaO ( s ) + CO ( g ) \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
